content:
**Subject.** This function reference covers reinstatement of an expired credit of Crestline Flight Credits and Travel Vouchers at the customer's request. Reinstatement gives the credit a new term of validity and books the value written off at its expiry back onto it.

## 1. Capability

The reinstatement function makes an expired credit usable again in one call:
- the credit's status becomes active;
- its reinstatement count goes up by one and its closing date is cleared;
- its new expiry date is set to the date of the call plus the whole number of calendar months supplied, keeping the same day of the month, or the last day of that month when the day does not exist;
- the value given is booked back onto the credit as one restore entry that points at the expiry write-off entry it undoes, and the credit's remaining value rises by that amount.

The customer then sees the credit as active, with the new expiry date and the restored balance.

## 2. How it is invoked

The function is `reinstate_credit_8e2d0d7d`. It is an agent-side tool: the agent unlocks it and then calls it on the customer's behalf.

## 3. Parameters

| Parameter | Meaning | Format |
|---|---|---|
| `credit_id` | Reference number of the credit record being reinstated, as returned by the credit lookup for this customer | identifier |
| `writeoff_entry_id` | Reference number of the expiry write-off entry on that credit that the restore undoes, as returned by the credit value-history lookup | identifier |
| `restored_value` | The value booked back onto the credit, in US dollars | non-negative amount with 2 decimal places, rounded to the nearest cent on receipt |
| `validity_months` | The new term of validity, in whole calendar months | integer of at least 1; the new expiry date is the date of the call moved forward by that many months |

## 4. Return

On success the function returns a receipt with these keys:
- `status` — the word "reinstated";
- `credit_id` — the credit reinstated;
- `restore_entry_id` — reference number of the new restore entry;
- `restored_value` — the amount booked back, in US dollars with 2 decimal places;
- `new_expiry_date` — the new expiry date, in the form YYYY-MM-DD;
- `reinstatement_count` — the credit's reinstatement count after the call, an integer;
- `remaining_value` — the credit's remaining value after the restore, in US dollars with 2 decimal places.

## Input and receipt illustrated (hypothetical values)

Suppose a call is made on 31 August 2026 with a restored value of 212.30 and a term of 6 months, on a credit whose remaining value was 0.00 and whose reinstatement count was 0. The receipt then shows status "reinstated", restored value 212.30, new expiry date 2027-02-28 (31 February does not exist), reinstatement count 1 and remaining value 212.30. The values are illustrations of the function's contract, not recommended inputs.
